00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.650
You can't protect them entirely from risky
behaviors and nor should you, in some

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.150
ways, because it's part of growing up.

00:00:11.236 --> 00:00:13.726
It's part of how they learn and
it's part of how they develop.

00:00:13.816 --> 00:00:16.939
And I think again, I keep going back
to my previous experience, but I think

00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:19.579
what happens with young people who are
in foster care or young people that are

00:00:19.579 --> 00:00:24.589
in residential homes, is that there's
more of a microscope on them because

00:00:24.589 --> 00:00:28.664
it's kind of, even if the foster carer
or the people in the home are great

00:00:28.724 --> 00:00:32.894
and love the kid and really want to be
there for them, they are still being

00:00:32.894 --> 00:00:34.514
employed and they are being watched.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:41.614
So I think there can be a tendency to stop
any risky behavior and just cut it off.

00:00:42.184 --> 00:00:44.979
And I think that that can be really
damaging in some ways 'cause I

00:00:44.979 --> 00:00:48.339
think that maybe kids don't get to
experiment, they don't get to grow.

00:00:48.369 --> 00:00:51.459
And also in some other ways they
might just go and do more risky

00:00:51.459 --> 00:00:54.354
stuff behind their parents' back.

00:00:54.774 --> 00:00:58.804
The best way to sort of support and
manage those risky behaviors is again,

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:03.474
it's just some openness and some honesty
it's like, you know, to talk to your

00:01:03.474 --> 00:01:08.844
kid about these things and not sort
of shy away from them without judgment

00:01:08.904 --> 00:01:10.224
and without telling them off for them.

00:01:10.292 --> 00:01:12.302
That's the thing that really
surprises some young people when

00:01:12.302 --> 00:01:15.814
they come to sessions with us is
this thing of they might tell you

00:01:15.814 --> 00:01:18.304
they've done something and you might
tell them, "oh God, that doesn't,

00:01:18.304 --> 00:01:19.534
you know, that sounds a bit risky".

00:01:20.059 --> 00:01:23.389
But you're not judging them and
you're not telling them off and

00:01:23.389 --> 00:01:25.849
I think they expect that because
that's kind of what they've learned.

00:01:25.849 --> 00:01:27.289
So I think it's just openness.

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:28.309
It's honesty.

00:01:28.309 --> 00:01:33.019
It's sort of, talking to them about
the risks, but also showing some

00:01:33.019 --> 00:01:35.209
understanding and not just punishing them.

00:01:35.269 --> 00:01:39.229
I think punishing people is the
worst thing you can do in some

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:43.429
respects, obviously there's levels
where you have to, but I think to

00:01:43.429 --> 00:01:47.905
just sort of automatically punish
risky behavior when it's just part

00:01:47.905 --> 00:01:50.515
of teenage life isn't really helpful.

